**Read:** [The fluid mechanics of aerosols — Goodarz Ahmadi, Clarkson](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0), coagulation equation 11; compare [Aerosols and coagulation — Chiang C. Mei, MIT](../sources/Aerosols-and-Coagulation-Mei.lingua#r_433KSJ2E60YP29TNWRQV1Z7PS9), equations 2.7.28–2.7.32.

With N particles in a well-mixed volume V, there are N(N−1)/2 distinct unordered pairs. If each pair has encounter rate β/V and every encounter merges two into one, total count decreases at event rate βN(N−1)/(2V). For a large population with number density n, the mean-field equation is dn/dt=−βn²/2.

For constant β, n(t)=n₀/[1+βn₀t/2], and the half-time is 2/(βn₀). Some sources call K=β/2 the coagulation constant, in which case the loss is −Kn². Always identify which constant is defined. Using the pair encounter kernel directly in −βn² double-counts the events.

**Work:** Enumerate the unordered pairs for four labelled particles and derive the finite-volume event count before taking a large-N approximation. Resolve the inconsistent constants in Mei's notes against both this enumeration and Ahmadi's equation. Explain why a constant-kernel law is a model rather than an automatic description of a changing size distribution.

